/-!
# Reflections — runnable proof-engineering demonstrations

This library collects the self-contained (core Lean, no `L4YAML` import) runnable
illustrations of the proof-engineering principles documented as numbered *Reflections* in
`Blueprint/08-initiative-4-intrinsic-foundations.md`.  They are kept separate from the
behavioural L4YAML test suites (parsing/emitting/round-trip) because they assert *methodology*,
not library behaviour: each is a toy model whose `#guard`/`#guard_msgs`/`#eval`/`by decide`
checks fail the build if the principle's positive/negative witnesses ever drift.

Each module names the Blueprint Reflection it illustrates:
* `ComplementGuardNotSufficient`          — Reflection 333 (the COMPLEMENT of a sufficient dispatch guard need not be a sufficient guard — the toy of `recseqbody_descend`, the DESCEND arm. A spine-walk splits into ADVANCE (`p` past the head entry) and DESCEND (`p` inside it). The ADVANCE guard `bal lo p = 0 ∧ lo < p` is SUFFICIENT for "past the head"; the NAIVE negation `bal lo p ≥ 1` is NOT sufficient for "inside the head" — `p` may be nested in a LATER entry (floor-blind, [[ref-floor-blind-gate-admits-cross-sibling]] on the complement side). The real DESCEND guard is the window-absolute FLOOR `∀ i ∈ (lo,p], bal lo i ≥ 1`. Witness `1 , [ [ 2 ] ]` (interior of `[1,[[2]]]`): a SCALAR head `1` (span `[0,1)`), then a doubly-nested seq. NEGATIVE (`#guard`): `bal 0 3 ≥ 1` holds so the negated guard fires, yet `p = 3 ≥ headEnd = 1` (past the head) and the FLOOR fails — `bal 0 1 = 0` (the head's return-to-`0`); the floor is the discriminator the bare `≥ 1` lacks. NEGATIVE (`#guard`): `delta op = delta mop` — a seq opener and a map opener share delta `+1`, so head-is-`.seq` is provably NOT a balance fact and enters as the dispatch hypothesis `h_lo_open`, not a derivation. POSITIVE (proven `floor_implies_in_head`, the crux of `recseqbody_descend`'s `h_p_lt`): abstractly over any `B`, if the head balances to `0` at its end `m` (`B m = 0`, `lo < m`) and the floor holds over `(lo,p]`, then `p < m` — the floor at `i = m` would force `B m ≥ 1`, contra `= 0`. The guard/complement analogue of [[ref-converse-forward-invariant-asymmetry]]; applies [[ref-window-absolute-gate-subset-restriction]] / [[ref-minimal-pair-extracts-the-gate]])
* `AdvanceArmBalanceFree`                  — Reflection 334 (the GENERAL ADVANCE arm is BALANCE-FREE once the caller supplies the decomposition — the toy of `recseqbody_advance_general`. R332's `recseqbody_advance` guarded advancing past the head with a BALANCE fact `bal lo p = 0` and spent its bulk USING that balance to RECONSTRUCT the structural `p ≥ lo + e.length`. R334 finding: the balance was never intrinsic to advancing — the CALLER already extracts the head entry and dispatches on `p ≥ lo + e.length`, so it already holds that inequality AND the `cons` decomposition `body = head ++ sep :: rest`; pass them DOWN and the balance machinery EVAPORATES, leaving pure slice algebra + one opener-vs-separator inequality. Witness `1 , [ [ 2 ] ]`: SCALAR head `1` (`headEnd = 1`), then a doubly-nested seq. NEGATIVE (`#guard`): `bal 0 3 = 1 ≠ 0` so the balance route cannot advance at the nested `p = 3`, yet `p ≥ headEnd` (the structural route can); the separator/opener tokens differ (`tok headEnd = .comma ≠ .op = tok 3`) so `p ≠ headEnd` is a CONSTRUCTOR inequality, not a balance fact. POSITIVE (proven `advance_rebase`): GIVEN `head ++ sep :: rest`, the re-base `(head ++ sep :: rest).drop (headLen + 1) = rest` is pure `List.drop_append` — no balance, no floor. POSITIVE (proven `sep_ne_opener`): if `body[headLen]? = some sep`, `body[p]? = some opener`, `sep ≠ opener`, then `p ≠ headLen`. The upstream dual of [[ref-reconstruct-in-place-over-relocate]]; resolution pattern for [[ref-complement-guard-not-sufficient]]; an [[ref-additive-parallel-type-over-shared-edit]] sibling)
* `SeveredEdgeBoundsNavigator`           — Reflection 335 (a SEVERED recursion edge bounds a navigator's domain; a PATH-blind gate admits windows past it — the toy of the `SeqMapPathNestedProbe` de-risk that REFUTED `rec_seq_body_nested_project`'s totality. A producer navigating a recursive deliverable can only reach windows whose PATH lies within the type's STORED edges; a member that PROJECTS rather than STORES the sub-deliverable (`RecSeqEntry.map` carries `WellBracketed`, not `RecSeqBody`) SEVERS the navigation. The consumer gate is PATH-BLIND (pins the IMMEDIATE enclosure, not the path), so it admits a map-PATH-nested seq window the navigator can't inhabit — invisible to a probe that only descends the navigable case; probe the PATH. Toy `Body`: `.seq` STORES the inner `Body`, `.map` PROJECTS to a weaker `Flat`. NEGATIVE (`#guard`): `bodyInner? (.map (.wrap .leaf)) = none` (navigator dies at the severed edge) though `flatIsSeqHead (.wrap .leaf) = true` (gate passes); `map_never_navigable`/`seq_always_navigable` show the severed edge is intrinsic to the constructor. POSITIVE (proven `flatProvider`): serve the gate-passing target from the WEAKER `Flat` substrate — the fix is RESTRICT the navigator + a flat provider, NOT strengthen the type. Sharpens [[ref-stored-vs-projected-severs-recursion-edge]] + [[ref-probe-provider-head-blind-gate]]; totality-refuting variant of [[ref-downstream-derisk-restores-upstream]])
* `AggregateCollapsesStructuredSeparates` — Reflection 336 (the dispatch is the un-aggregated STRUCTURED STATE, not a numeric aggregate of it — the toy of `SeqPathDispatchProbe`. After a [[ref-severed-edge-bounds-navigator-domain]] de-risk refutes a navigator's totality, find the DISPATCH partitioning the path-blind gate's windows into navigable vs unreachable by probing a numeric AGGREGATE against the un-aggregated STRUCTURED STATE it was folded from, over a minimal pair. Toy: a typed-frame `Stack`; `depth` is the `Nat` aggregate (the `flowBracketBalance` analogue), `pathAllSeq` reads the whole stack (the `btFold` analogue), `topFrame` is the gate's path-blind projection. NEGATIVE (`#guard` + `aggregate_collapses`): the map-path stack `[true,false,true]` and all-seq `[true,true,true]` have EQUAL `depth` 3 and EQUAL `topFrame` `some true` — the aggregate COLLAPSES the pair (REJECTED; the collapse IS the necessity leg). POSITIVE (`#guard` + `example`): `pathAllSeq` is `false` vs `true` — the buried map frame SEPARATES them; the structured state read WHOLE is the dispatch, the gate's `topFrame` only its head-projection. Applies [[ref-minimal-pair-extracts-the-gate]] to a PATH-dispatch; discharges the reshaped next step of [[ref-severed-edge-bounds-navigator-domain]])
* `DomainRestrictionPreservationNegation` — Reflection 337 (de-risk a navigator's DOMAIN-RESTRICTION with a PRESERVATION/NEGATION pair — the toy of `SeqPathAllSeq`'s descend lemmas. When a severed edge bounds a navigator's domain ([[ref-severed-edge-bounds-navigator-domain]]) and you RESTRICT the domain to fence it out, prove BOTH that the KEPT edge maps the domain into itself AND that the EXCLUDED edge maps it OUT — it is the NEGATION that licenses dropping the excluded recursion arm, not the preservation alone. Toy: a typed-frame `Stack`, `InDomain` = nonempty all-`true` (the `SeqPathAllSeq` analogue), `pushSeq`/`pushMap` the two edges. POSITIVE (proven `preservation`): `pushSeq` keeps an all-`true` stack in domain (the seq head discharges the restriction — `seqPathAllSeq_descend`). NEGATIVE (proven `negation`): `pushMap` BREAKS it (a map head is out-of-domain ⇒ no fifth map-mirror arm — `seqPathAllSeq_map_push_breaks`). `dominance`: the domain implies TOP `true` (`seqEnclosed_of_seqPathAllSeq`). `#guard`s pin the real minimal pair's stacks `[true,false,true]` (out) vs `[true,true,true]` (in). Lifts [[ref-converse-forward-invariant-asymmetry]] / [[ref-push-blind-frame-dependent]] from the TOP to the whole structured state [[ref-aggregate-collapses-structured-separates]])
* `ProducerStopsAtSeveredEdge` — Reflection 338 (a PRODUCER stops at a severed edge as a leaf; a NAVIGATOR dies there — the toy behind `rec_seq_body_nested_project`'s one-line landing. When a severed/projecting edge makes a NAVIGATOR non-total ([[ref-severed-edge-bounds-navigator-domain]]), check whether the deliverable can be PRODUCED instead of navigated before restricting the domain. Toy: a `Carrier` tree whose `box` member stores only a flat `Bool` (no recursive child — the `RecSeqEntry.map` analogue). NEGATIVE (proven `severed_navigation_stuck`): `navigate` DIES at a `box` window — it must descend INTO the member and there is no child (R335). POSITIVE (proven `severed_production_total`): `produce` STOPS AT the box, emits the leaf, recurses no further — TOTAL where navigation dies (`seqWindowRecSeqBody`'s near-leaf map oracle). `kept_edge_agrees`: the two agree on the KEPT seq edge, diverging only at the severed one. `#guard`s pin the divergence: a box-buried window navigates to `none` but produces `.box true`. For a `Prop` deliverable the two are interchangeable (proof-irrelevance) ⇒ prefer production, it sidesteps the edge; the domain restriction survives only as the consumer's routing tag ([[ref-coerce-to-weaker-reuse-wrapper]]), the four navigate-around arms an alternative not a necessity)
* `ProbeRefutesComplementProvider` — Reflection 339 (a probe of a would-be COMPLEMENT provider REFUTES its necessity — the toy behind `seqEnclosed_of_seqTypedInterior`'s one-line landing. When a navigator framing demands a domain PARTITION + a second provider for the excluded windows, PROBE before authoring: is the producer even sensitive to the partitioning predicate, is its real need PATH-INVARIANT, and does the consumer's GATE definitionally contain it. Toy: a `Stack` of typed frames; `Enclosed` = the HEAD is a seq (path-invariant); `StrongPath` = the WHOLE stack all-seq; `Gate` whose `enc` field IS `Enclosed`. BRIDGE (proven `enclosed_of_gate := g.enc`): the gate supplies the producer's need in one line ([[ref-conjunct-of-projection-is-free-field]] at the gate). PROBE (proven `probe_not_strongPath` + `probeGate`): the window `[true, false]` FAILS `StrongPath` (a `false` deeper down) yet the `Gate` HOLDS — so the producer serves the `¬StrongPath` window through the gate, NO complement needed. `#guard`s pin it: `probeStack.all (·==true) = false` but `probeStack.head? = some true`. So `StrongPath` is VESTIGIAL and ONE producer serves the whole gated domain — the size-down dual of [[ref-downstream-derisk-restores-upstream]] (the discriminator was never needed; the gate carried it))
* `EndFreeGateUnderdeterminesClose` — Reflection 340 (an END-FREE gate underdetermines a window's CLOSE — the toy behind the `(i'-b-B2c-provider-from-gate)` probe `SeqGateWindownessProbe`. A provider quantified under a gate whose conjuncts constrain only window-INTERNAL invariants (balance, enclosure mark, Dyck floor — none mentioning the token AT the end `b`) cannot read its structural close off `get s b`. Toy: `Tok := op | cl | it | sep`; `bal`/`windowBal` over a slice; an END-free `Gate` with one balance-`0` conjunct. PROBE (proven `gate_24` + `end_24_not_close`): the `b ≠ close` window `[2,4)` = `it sep` (mirror of `"1" ,`) on `W = op op it sep it cl sep it cl` (mirror of `[[1,2],9]`) PASSES the gate yet `get W 4 = it`, NOT `cl`. BRIDGE (proven `located_close_24`): the close is read off the LOCATED enclosing structure `hiS = 5` (`get W 5 = cl`), the window SELECTED inside by `4 ≤ 5` — never off `b`, mirroring `seqDescent_provider_of_located`. `#guard`s pin it: `windowBal W 2 4 = 0` but `get W 4 ≠ cl`, while `get W 5 = cl`. END-dual of [[ref-probe-provider-head-blind-gate]] (head-blind admits spurious-HEADED windows; end-free admits spurious-ENDED ones); the existing satisfiability probe MASKED it by only using `b = close` windows. SHARPENED by Reflection 356 (`gate_admits_three_ends`/`close_token_separates_three_ends` on `V = op it sep it sep it cl`): a SETTLED next-step pointer (the emission-spine wrapper `nestedSeq_recseqentry_locate`, R355) re-credited balance-0 with pinning `b`; re-running the probe shows the gate admits not one but ARBITRARILY MANY non-close ends (a seq of `n` elements has `n-1` separator ends, all balance-0 + floored), so `b` is pinned by the consumer's explicit close hypothesis `tokens[hi]!.val = .flowSequenceEnd` — `seqWindowRecSeqBody`'s fourth `G`-conjunct — not by the interior gate; re-probe a boundary-pinning attribution even when a settled pointer states it)
